<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christiansted</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christiansted is the largest town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a>. It grew from the French settlement of Bassin and was laid out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> after they bought the island from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1733, becoming the capital of the Danish West Indies from 1755 to 1871. Its colonial waterfront buildings form a national historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christiansted.jpg" alt="Christiansted" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Megatron19 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christiansted-1-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Christiansted is the largest town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a>, one of the main islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a>. Its name is Danish for Christian's Place, after King Christian VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The streets run at right angles to the waterfront, and the commercial area centres on King and Company streets, beside the Christiansted National Historic Site. The residential area, which includes parts first settled by free Black people, extends inland and uphill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Captain Frederik Moth founded the town after he became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St. Croix</a> in 1733. His party, which set out from St. Thomas, had cleared a space for Fort Christianswærn by 5 September. On 8 January 1734, in a ceremony beside the fort, the French formally handed the island over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> in the person of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-west-india-and-guinea-company">Danish West India and Guinea Company</a>. The island was to be divided into 300 plantations, 215 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and the rest for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and the company also set up a sugar refinery and distillery. The fort was finished by 1740.</p><p>The 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> listed 120 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, 122 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantations, 1,906 enslaved people, about 300 Englishmen and 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. By 1745 the enslaved population had grown to 2,878, and by 1754 it numbered 7,566. The 83 white inhabitants of the town in that year each owned between one and sixty-six enslaved people. The town was built by African slaves, which is why its design carries African as well as Danish influences. Later residents included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a> in 1765 and, from 1887 to 1905, the fireburn leader Mary Thomas, who was imprisoned there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Christiansted keeps 18th-century Danish-style stone buildings in pastel colours with red tile roofs along cobblestone pavements. It was a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>. Tourism supports its economy, and Henry E. Rohlsen Airport serves it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christiansted, U.S. Virgin Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony centred on a small settlement at Bassin, later <b>Christiansted</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-croix">French Saint Croix</a></li></ul></section>
